---
import Divider from "../../../../components/ui/Divider.astro";
import Layout from "../../../../layouts/Layout.astro";
import { Code as CodeSnippet } from "astro:components";
---

<Layout>
  <div class="container my-4">
    <div class="row justify-content-center">
      <div class="col-sm-12 col-md-10">
        <a href="/courses/ap-csp/" class="btn btn-sm btn-outline-light mb-3">
          <i class="si-arrow-left"></i> Back
        </a>

        <h1>Section Summary: Computer Systems and Networks</h1>
        <Divider variant="8" classes="mb-4" />

        <p class="text-secondary">
          Activities 3.6 &ndash; 3.10. Use this to review, or to fill in a definition you missed.
        </p>

        <hr />

        <section>
          <h2><span class="text-sn-theme">1.</span> Devices, Systems, and Networks</h2>

          <p>
            Three words that nest inside
            one another. Data travels a path between them, and bandwidth is a
            measured number rather than a
            description.
          </p>

          <dl>
            <dt>Computing device</dt>
            <dd>A physical artifact that can run a program.</dd>
            <dt>Computing system</dt>
            <dd>A group of computing devices and programs working together for a common purpose.</dd>
            <dt>Computer network</dt>
            <dd>A group of interconnected computing devices capable of sending or receiving data. A type of computing system.</dd>
            <dt>Path</dt>
            <dd>A sequence of directly connected computing devices beginning at the sender and ending at the receiver.</dd>
            <dt>Routing</dt>
            <dd>The process of finding a path from sender to receiver.</dd>
            <dt>Bandwidth</dt>
            <dd>The maximum amount of data that can be sent in a fixed amount of time, usually measured in bits per second.</dd>
          </dl>
        </section>

        <hr />

        <section>
          <h2><span class="text-sn-theme">2.</span> The Internet, and Why It Could Grow</h2>

          <p>
            The internet works
            because everyone agreed on the same published rules and nobody owns
            them. That is what let it scale.
          </p>

          <dl>
            <dt>Protocol</dt>
            <dd>An agreed-upon set of rules that specify the behavior of a system.</dd>
            <dt>Open protocol</dt>
            <dd>Non-proprietary. The rules are published, and anyone may implement them.</dd>
            <dt>Scalability</dt>
            <dd>The capacity for a system to change in size and scale to meet new demands.</dd>
            <dt>Dynamic routing</dt>
            <dd>Paths are worked out as data moves, not specified in advance.</dd>
          </dl>
        </section>

        <hr />

        <section>
          <h2><span class="text-sn-theme">3.</span> Packets, and the Web That Is Not the Internet</h2>

          <p>
            Data is broken
            into packets that travel independently and arrive out of order. The
            web runs on the internet; it is not the same
            thing.
          </p>

          <dl>
            <dt>Data stream</dt>
            <dd>Information passed through the internet as chunks of data.</dd>
            <dt>Packet</dt>
            <dd>A chunk of data plus metadata used for routing and for reassembly.</dd>
            <dt>IP</dt>
            <dd>A protocol responsible for addressing and routing.</dd>
            <dt>TCP</dt>
            <dd>A protocol that breaks data into packets and re-requests packets that do not arrive.</dd>
            <dt>UDP</dt>
            <dd>A protocol that sends without checking for missing packets.</dd>
            <dt>World Wide Web</dt>
            <dd>A system of linked pages, programs, and files, which uses the internet. HTTP is one of its protocols.</dd>
          </dl>
        </section>

        <hr />

        <section>
          <h2><span class="text-sn-theme">4.</span> Fault Tolerance and Redundancy</h2>

          <p>
            Extra paths let a network
            keep working when part of it fails. Look for the component whose
            loss would cut the system in two.
          </p>

          <dl>
            <dt>Redundancy</dt>
            <dd>The inclusion of extra components that can be used to mitigate failure of a system if other components fail.</dd>
            <dt>Fault tolerant</dt>
            <dd>Able to support failures and still continue to function.</dd>
            <dt>Single point of failure</dt>
            <dd>A component whose failure disconnects part of the system. The thing redundancy removes.</dd>
          </dl>
        </section>

        <hr />

        <section>
          <h2><span class="text-sn-theme">5.</span> Sequential, Parallel, and Distributed</h2>

          <p>
            Doing steps at the
            same time is faster than doing them one after another &mdash; up to
            the point where the sequential part is all that is
            left.
          </p>

          <dl>
            <dt>Sequential computing</dt>
            <dd>Operations performed in order, one at a time. Takes as long as the sum of all its steps.</dd>
            <dt>Parallel computing</dt>
            <dd>The program broken into smaller sequential operations, some performed simultaneously.</dd>
            <dt>Distributed computing</dt>
            <dd>Multiple devices used to run a program.</dd>
            <dt>Speedup</dt>
            <dd>Sequential run time divided by parallel run time.</dd>
          </dl>
        </section>
      </div>
    </div>
  </div>
</Layout>

<style>
  dl {
    margin-bottom: 0;
  }
  dt {
    margin-top: 0.75rem;
  }
  dd {
    margin-bottom: 0;
    padding-left: 1rem;
  }
  section {
    margin-bottom: 2rem;
  }
  code {
    font-size: 1rem;
  }
</style>
